\c 1
\s1 God’s command to rebuild the temple
\p
\v 1 In Dareyavesh’s \add (Darius)\add* second year as king \add of Paras\add* \add (Persia)\add*, on the 1st of the sixth month, the prophet Hagay brought Yahweh’s message to the governor of Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*, Zerubavel (Shealtiyel’s son), and to the high priest, Yehoshua \add (Joshua)\add* (Yehotsadak’s son),\x + \xo 1:1: \xt Ezr 4:24–5:2; 6:14.\x* telling \add them that\add*
\v 2 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, “These people say that \add it’s not the right\add* time to rebuild Yahweh’s \add residence\add*.”
\p
\v 3 Then Yahweh \add gave this message\add* to the prophet Hagay \add to tell the people\add*:
\m
\v 4 Is it a time for all of you to live in your panelled houses, while \add Yahweh’s temple lies in ruins\add*?
\v 5 \add So\add* now Commander-in-chief Yahweh says: “\add Decide what you’re all going to do\add*.
\v 6 You’ve all planted a lot, \add but only harvested a little. You’ve eaten, but it never fills you. You all drink, but never enough to satisfy you. You put on clothes, but never feel warm enough. You earn wages, but your pockets seem to be full of holes\add*.”
\p
\v 7 \add So\add* Commander-in-chief Yahweh says \add again\add*: “\add Decide what you’re all going to do\add*.
\v 8 Go up into the hills and bring back timber to build the \add temple\add*. This will please and honour me,” says Yahweh.
\p
\v 9 “You \add expected\add* much, but \add gained\add* little. Anything you brought home, I blew away \add again\add*. Why? Commander-in-chief Yahweh \add says\add* it’s because my residence is still in ruins, while you’re all \add busy\add* \add working on\add* \add your\add* own houses.
\v 10 That’s why the sky withholds the dew and the soil withholds its crops.
\v 11 I’ve \add summoned\add* a drought onto the land and into the hills, onto the grain and the new wine, onto the oil and crops from the ground, onto \add both\add* people and livestock, and onto \add everything you all do\add*.”
\s1 The people start rebuilding
\p
\v 12 Then Shealtiyel’s son Zerubavel and Yehotsadak’s son Yehoshua \add (Joshua)\add*, the high priest, and all the rest of the people listened to the voice of their god Yahweh \add via\add* the words of the prophet Hagay, because Yahweh their god had sent him, and the people \add respected\add* Yahweh.
\v 13 Then Yahweh’s messenger Hagay passed this message from Yahweh onto the people, “Yahweh \add declares\add* that I’m with you all.”
\v 14 Then Yahweh \add inspired\add* Shealtiyel’s son Zerubavel, the governor of Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*, \add inspired\add* Yehotsadak’s son Yehoshua, the high priest, and \add inspired\add* all the rest of the people, and they came and \add started\add* work on the \add temple\add* for their god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh,
\v 15 on the twenty-fourth day of the sixth month of the second year of Dareyavesh \add (Darius)\add* the king \add of Paras\add* \add (Persia)\add*.
\c 2
\s1 The splendour of the new temple
\p
\v 1 On the 21st of the seventh month \add (about a month later)\add*, Yahweh \add spoke\add* \add again\add* through the prophet Hagay:
\v 2 Please \add ask\add* Shealtiyel’s son Zerubavel, the governor of Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*, and \add ask\add* Yehotsadak’s son Yehoshua \add (Joshua)\add*, the high priest, and \add ask\add* the rest of the people,
\v 3 “\add Are there any of you still alive who saw the splendour of the former temple\add*? How does it look to you now? \add It must now seem pretty much like \+em nothing\+em* in comparison\add*.\x + \xo 2:3: \xt Ezr 3:12.\x*
\v 4 Yahweh is telling you now, Zerubavel, to be strong. And be strong, high priest Yehoshua, and be strong all you people of the land. Commander-in-chief Yahweh \add declares\add* that I’m with you \add as you work\add*.
\v 5 \add That’s what I promised your ancestors when they\add* came out of Mitsrayim \add (Egypt)\add*, and my spirit remains \add among you\add*.\x + \xo 2:5: \xt Exo 29:45-46.\x* Don’t be afraid,
\v 6 because Commander-in-chief Yahweh says that in a little while, I’ll shake the heavens and the earth, the sea and the dry land, once more.\x + \xo 2:6: \xt Heb 12:26.\x*
\v 7 I’ll shake all the nations, and they’ll come \add here bringing\add* their treasure. Then I’ll fill this \add temple\add* with \add my\add* splendour, says Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\v 8 Commander-in-chief Yahweh \add declares\add* that the gold and silver \add belong\add* to me.
\v 9 \add I\add* declare that this \add temple\add* will be \add greater in the future than it was in the past\add*, and \add also\add* that I will give peace and prosperity to this place.”
\s1 Hagay consults the priests
\p
\v 10 On the 24th of the ninth month in \add King\add* Dareyavesh’s \add (Darius)\add* second year \add (about two months later)\add*, Yahweh \add gave a message\add* to the prophet Hagay:
\v 11 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, “Ask the priests about \add Mosheh’s\add* \add (Moses)\add* \add instructions\add*.
\v 12 ‘\add If a priest took some meat that had been offered to God and carried it wrapped in a piece of clothing, then if the clothing touched some other food, would that other food\add* become holy?’ ”
\p “No, it wouldn’t,” the priests \add replied\add*.
\p
\v 13 Then Hagay asked, “\add But\add* if a person became unclean by touching a corpse and then touched any of that \add food\add*, would it become unclean?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* it would become unclean,”\x + \xo 2:13: \xt Num 19:11-22.\x* the priests answered.
\p
\v 14 “\add That’s what Yahweh declares about you\add* people,” Hagay \add continued\add*. “\add He says that\add* \add that’s how this country acts towards him\add*. \add Your actions are dishonourable, and then that same disrespect transfers to your offerings\add*.
\v 15 \add So\add* now think back to before stones were being laid for Yahweh’s temple.
\v 16 \add During that time\add*, when someone went to get twenty \add containers of grain\add*, there were \add only\add* ten there, and when someone went to fill fifty \add jars of wine\add* from the vat, there was \add only enough for\add* twenty.
\v 17 Yahweh \add declares\add* that \add he\add* \add caused\add* blight and mildew and hail to affect your work, \add but\add* you \add still\add* didn’t turn to \add him\add*.
\v 18 Think back to the time from when the \add foundation\add* of Yahweh’s temple was laid, until today (this 24th of the ninth month). Consider that.
\v 19 Is \add any grain\add* left in the storehouse for seed? \add What’s more,\add* the \add vines\add*, and the fig trees and pomegranate trees and olive trees, haven’t produced fruit. \add However,\add* \add Yahweh\add* will bless you from today \add onwards\add*.”
\s1 God’s promise to Zerubavel
\p
\v 20 Then Yahweh gave a second message to Hagay on the 24th:
\v 21 Tell Zerubavel, the governor of Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*, “I’m \add about to\add* shake the heavens and the earth.
\v 22 I’ll overthrow the \add thrones\add* of kingdoms, and destroy the strength of the nations. I’ll overthrow chariots and riders—horses and their riders will fall and \add related tribes\add* \add will kill each other\add*.
\v 23 Commander-in-chief Yahweh \add declares\add* that on that day \add he\add* will take Shealtiyel’s son Zerubavel and \add cause him to place Yahweh’s mark on the nation\add* like a signet ring, because \add he’s\add* been chosen.”
